title: Graphics profiles
description: Backend prerequisites, generated targets, SDK configuration, and rendering test behavior.
section: Reference
order: 15
updated: '2026-10-14'
---

C++ graphics profiles add a triangle renderer, shaders, and a runtime pixel test. The generated executable is `<name>_<profile>`; its CTest is `<name>_<profile>_runtime`. Project creation leaves SDK installation to you. For the common creation and task workflow, see [Your first C++ project](/docs/first-project/).

## Backends

| Profile | Rendering path | Prerequisites |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `metal` | Metal-cpp, MSL, offscreen texture/readback | macOS, Xcode Metal tools, Metal-cpp headers |
| `webgpu` | Native Dawn C++ API, WGSL, offscreen render/readback | C++20+, installed Dawn CMake package |
| `vulkan` | Vulkan pipeline, GLSL compiled to SPIR-V, offscreen readback | Vulkan headers/loader and `glslc`; validation layers for Debug |
| `opengl` | OpenGL 3.3 core context, GLFW, GLSL 330 | GLFW 3.3+, OpenGL development libraries, usable display/context |

The host standard and project-module choices are independent of the profile. This example selects C++23 with headers:

```sh
rivet new graphics_demo --profile metal --standard 23 --modules false
```

## Local SDK configuration

SDK paths belong in a local configure preset. This Metal example can be added to `CMakeUserPresets.json`; merge with any existing presets and replace the paths:

```json
{
  "version": 6,
  "configurePresets": [{
    "name": "graphics-debug",
    "inherits": "debug",
    "binaryDir": "${sourceDir}/build/graphics-debug",
    "cacheVariables": {
      "CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER": "/usr/bin/clang++",
      "RIVET_METAL_CPP_DIR": "/path/to/metal-cpp"
    }
  }]
}
```

Other backends use the cache variables below. Select a compiler compatible with the SDK and use a new build directory when changing toolchains.

| Backend | Discovery and relevant settings |
| --- | --- |
| Metal | `RIVET_METAL_CPP_DIR`: root containing `Metal/Metal.hpp` and `Foundation/Foundation.hpp`; `xcrun` supplies the Metal compiler |
| WebGPU | `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH`: installed Dawn prefix with `DawnConfig.cmake` and `dawn::webgpu_dawn` |
| Vulkan | `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH`: SDK prefix when needed; `Vulkan_GLSLC_EXECUTABLE`: shader compiler path |
| OpenGL | `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH`: GLFW installation prefix when it is outside normal search paths |

## Metal

The build compiles `shaders/triangle.metal` into `triangle.metallib`. Run prints `Metal center pixel: R,G,B`; the check requires red greater than 150 and alpha greater than 200. A missing Metal device returns 77; shader-library and pipeline errors fail.

Apple's [Metal-cpp setup guide](https://developer.apple.com/metal/cpp/) provides the headers and toolchain requirements. `xcrun --find metal` must resolve before building.

## Native WebGPU

The profile links an installed Dawn package and uses its callback/future C++ API. A source checkout alone does not satisfy `find_package(Dawn CONFIG REQUIRED)`. See [Dawn's CMake quickstart](https://dawn.googlesource.com/dawn/+/refs/heads/main/docs/quickstart-cmake.md) for installation and exported targets.

Run prints `WebGPU center pixel: R,G,B` and checks the same red/alpha thresholds as Metal. No adapter or a null backend returns 77. Device creation, shader loading, and readback failures fail. Browser builds require a separate web toolchain and application integration.

## Vulkan

The build uses `glslc` to compile the GLSL shaders to SPIR-V. Run prints `Vulkan center pixel: R,G,B` and checks red greater than 150 and alpha greater than 200. Debug requires `VK_LAYER_KHRONOS_validation` and fails on reported validation errors; Release does not enable that layer.

An incompatible driver, missing physical device, or missing graphics queue returns 77. Other Vulkan errors fail. The starter enables portability enumeration and the portability-subset extension when advertised.

The loader, drivers, and layers must be available in the runtime environment. On macOS, a MoltenVK installation supplies Vulkan over Metal. See [CMake Vulkan discovery](https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/module/FindVulkan.html) and the [MoltenVK user guide](https://github.com/KhronosGroup/MoltenVK/blob/main/Docs/MoltenVK_Runtime_UserGuide.md) for their respective setup.

## OpenGL

Run opens a GLFW window and continues until it closes. CTest passes `--test`, which selects a hidden window and an automatic exit. The generated program currently treats any argument as test mode.

The pixel check requires red greater than 150 and no OpenGL error. It prints no center-pixel line. GLFW initialization or context creation failure returns 77; shader and pixel errors fail. A hidden window still needs a usable context. The starter loads functions with `glfwGetProcAddress`, as described in [GLFW's context guidance](https://www.glfw.org/docs/latest/quick_guide.html).

## Tasks and test results

For the Metal example above:

```sh
rivet run --root graphics_demo --preset graphics-debug --target graphics_demo_metal
rivet test --root graphics_demo --preset graphics-debug
```

Run and Test configure and build first. In Neovim, `:RivetPreset` selects the configuration and `:RivetRunTarget` selects the profile executable.

CTest marks return code 77 as **Skipped** and can still exit successfully overall. Check the named runtime test to establish that rendering ran. Compilation, shader/pipeline, and pixel-check errors remain failures. Doctor checks prerequisites without rendering a frame.

Shaders live under `shaders/`; builds compile or copy changed resources as needed. Pixel assertions live in `examples/<profile>/main.cpp`. A deliberate color change may require updating the assertion. Resources are loaded from the configured build directory, so deployment must include them and adapt their paths. See [Compatibility](/docs/compatibility/) for verification scope.
